U₃ is the name of a cuneiform sign that functions like the “&” symbol in
English: a shorter way to write the word “and”. The capital letters in our
transcription show that this is the name of the sign, not the actual Hittite
pronunciation.

> ilan carrahhun
Nu luttāi šarraḫḫun.

- - - - -
Nu=za=kan ḫilamni anda ešun.

Nu apiya āškan U₃ luttāi ōḫḫun.
Nu GIŠ^ḫattalu āški ēšta.

Verbs: ōḫḫun, pāun, šarraḫḫun, dāḫḫun, tēḫḫun, katta tēḫḫun
                  <2> āppa                 
           <4> arkiwi ✧ <3> luttāi           
                 <1> āškan                 
                                       |      
                                     -(@)+(.) 
                                       :   |  
                                  ( )-( )-( ) 
                                       |      
                                      ( )     

Hittite nouns were sometimes written with a special sign in front, called a
“determiner” or “determinative”. These weren’t pronounced, but indicated some
property of the noun, like how “amber” is a rock but “Amber” is a person. GIŠ,
for example, marks that something is made of wood.

These signs are normally transcribed in superscript, but for typing, you can
use a ^ symbol instead (GIŠ^ḫattalu).

